+---
+id: task-001-give-each-task-a-unique-id
+type: task
+title: "Give each task a unique id"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Give each task a unique id
+
+## Request
+
+The app needs a stable way to refer to a task, one that still works after the task is renamed.
+
+- Each task has a unique identifier: a string that no other task of the list has.
+- `add` returns the new task with its `id`.
+- The list gets a `get(id)` method that returns the task with that id, as a result like the list's
+  other methods (`Result<Task>`, failing with `not-found` when no task has the id).
+- The methods that take a title (`find`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag`, `remove`)
+  also accept a task's id.
+
+## Feature and story
+
+- Feature: stable task references in the Tidy Tasks list.
+- Story: as an app screen, I refer to a task by its id, so that renaming the task does not break my reference.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+1. `add` returns a task whose `id` is a non-empty string.
+2. Two tasks in the same list never share an id, including tasks added after a removal.
+3. Renaming a task keeps its `id`; `get(id)` returns the renamed task.
+4. `get(id)` returns `ok` with the task for a known id, and `err('not-found', ...)` for an unknown id.
+5. `find`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag` and `remove` each accept an id in place of a
+   title and behave as they do with the title.
+6. A removed task's id is not given to a later task, so a stale id never resolves to a different task.
+7. Existing title-based behaviour is unchanged: titles are still trimmed, still unique, and still
+   reported as before.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- R1. `Task` carries an `id: string`, set when the task is created and never changed.
+- R2. `TaskList` gains `get(id: string): Result<Task>`.
+- R3. Each title-taking method resolves its argument as a task id when one matches it; otherwise as a
+  title, as today.
+- R4. Ids are generated without the clock or randomness, so that a given sequence of calls always gives
+  the same ids.
+- R5. The doc comments of the changed exports (`Task`, `TaskList`, `get`, and the title-taking methods)
+  say that an id may be given, and the `TaskList` doc comment no longer says tasks are only found by
+  title.
+- R6. The tests in `test/list.test.ts` cover the acceptance criteria above, and `npm test` passes.
+
+## Notes for the next steps
+
+- Choice made here: an argument that matches a task's id resolves to that task first; the title is used
+  only when no id matches. A title that happens to equal another task's id therefore resolves to that
+  other task; the app should avoid titles of that form, and nothing here prevents them.
+- Tests that compare a whole `Task` by `deepEqual` will need the new `id` field.
